.gitignore:34:docs/dev/	docs/dev/ARCHITECTURE.md
     1	# ARCHITECTURE
     2	
     3	The implementation of the strange-loops paradigm in Python. The paradigm is
     4	three shapes (Fact, Spec, Fold), four properties (append-only, participatory,
     5	boundary-driven, compositional), one pattern: facts flow in, folds accumulate,
     6	boundaries resolve, ticks flow out. This document is how and why those are
     7	instantiated as software. (Paradigm root statement:
     8	`observation:paradigm/strange-loops-root-statement` in the project store.)
     9	
    10	## Loops as protocol
    11	
    12	Loops is a **protocol** — a shape (`Fact`, `Tick`, `Cascade`, `Observer`,
    13	`Grant`) with operational properties (participatory observation, append-only
    14	storage, fold-derived state, boundary-driven ticks) — that any implementation
    15	can honor. This Python codebase is one instantiation; it is not the
    16	definition. The definition lives in the shape and the properties.
    17	
    18	Two consequences fall out:
    19	
    20	- **Same-language consumers** import the libraries directly. They share the
    21	  Python implementation, the conventions, the test infrastructure. The
    22	  protocol holds because the code holds.
    23	- **Cross-language consumers** implement the protocol over the wire — Facts
    24	  serialized as JSON, traversal verbs as a documented API, storage and
    25	  transport as their own concern. They share the shape without sharing
    26	  implementation. The protocol holds because the shape is portable.
    27	
    28	Both are valid; neither is privileged. Multiple implementations are expected
    29	in any sufficiently durable ecosystem — peer homelabs in different stacks,
    30	embedded subsystems with different constraints, alternate runtimes for
    31	research and replay. The shape survives across them because the shape is
    32	small, the substrate is thin, and the trust attaches to the fact-stream
    33	rather than to any specific implementation's enforcement.
    34	
    35	This framing is what makes the system trivially portable in a deeper sense
    36	than "the language is portable." The paradigm is portable; the paradigm is
    37	what matters. (The operational consequences are worked out in the
    38	trust-as-substrate essay, `docs/dev/essays/2026-05-13-on-trust-as-substrate.md`
    39	— a local dev note, not tracked here.)
    40	
    41	## Why Python
    42	
    43	Not because Python is fast, or because the ecosystem demands it. Because the
    44	developer can see the shape of the code and fold out expectations without
    45	reading it. Python is readable at a glance — the structure of a frozen
    46	dataclass, the signature of a pure function, the flow of a pipeline. When
    47	you're building a system for focusing attention, the implementation language
    48	needs to not fight the focus. Python gets out of the way.
    49	
    50	This also means the system is trivially portable. Everything is immutable,
    51	append-only, unidirectional. There's no mutable state to coordinate, no
    52	concurrency model baked in, no framework coupling. The Python is one
    53	instantiation. The paradigm is what matters.
    54	
    55	## Libraries
    56	
    57	Seven libraries in `libs/`. Each owns one concern. The runtime dependency graph is
    58	`_LIB_ALLOWED_RUNTIME` in `tests/test_architecture.py` — everything not listed
    59	there is forbidden, and the test is the authority:
    60	
    61	```
    62	atoms    ──→ ()
    63	lang     ──→ ()
    64	sign     ──→ ()
    65	engine   ──→ lang, atoms
    66	store    ──→ engine
    67	custody  ──→ sign, engine
    68	sdk      ──→ atoms, lang, sign, engine, store, custody
    69	```
    70	
    71	`X ──→ Y` reads *X imports Y at runtime*. painted is not in this graph — it
    72	lives in its own repo and arrives as a versioned PyPI dependency (see below).
    73	
    74	**atoms** — The ingress shapes as code. Fact and Spec as frozen dataclasses; the
    75	third shape, Tick, lives in engine, because a Tick is a runtime output rather
    76	than something you configure. Source as ingress configuration. Parse vocabulary (11 ops: Split, Pick, Rename, Coerce...) for
    77	shaping raw input. Fold vocabulary (10 ops: Latest, Count, Sum, Collect,
    78	Upsert...) for accumulating state. `Spec.apply(state, payload) → state` is pure
    79	— deep copies, folds, returns. Zero external dependencies, stdlib only
    80	(enforced by Rule 6).
    81	
    82	**engine** — The pattern as code. Vertex routes facts by kind to fold engines.
    83	Loop executes Spec.apply, tracks state, fires at boundaries. SqliteStore provides
    84	durable append-only persistence, the tick hash chain, and the signing injection
    85	points (`tick_signer`, `fact_signer`, `verify_chain`/`verify_facts` — callables
    86	in, never an import of the crypto). StoreReader provides read-only inspection.
    87	Peer/Grant provides identity and gating policy (implemented; no in-repo consumer
    88	yet — vouch is the forcing function). The compiler translates lang AST into
    89	runtime vertices. Engine imports lang at module scope for AST types and atoms
    90	through function-local lazy imports.
    91	
    92	**lang** — Configuration as code. KDL parser for `.loop` files (source
    93	definitions) and `.vertex` files (vertex configurations). Pure grammar — no
    94	runtime types, no execution. Produces frozen AST dataclasses. Validates shape
    95	inference through parse pipelines. The only external dependency is `ckdl`.
    96	
    97	**store** — Maintenance as code. Slice, merge, receive, compact, transport
    98	(push/pull), and rebirth for vertex store databases. Operates on the same SQLite
    99	databases that engine writes; it imports engine for `tick_row_hash` so chain
   100	hashing stays single-sourced. ULID primary keys make cross-database dedup trivial
   101	— same fact in two stores has the same ID, merge is `INSERT OR IGNORE`. Search is
   102	*not* here: FTS5 lives behind engine's vertex read interface.
   103	
   104	**sign** — Cryptographic primitives as code. RSA `KeyStore` with
   105	`load_or_generate`, JWT `mint`/`verify`, JWKS and OpenID-configuration document
   106	building, and `sign.ed25519` for detached digest signatures with domain
   107	separation. Deliberately loops-agnostic — it knows nothing of Fact, Tick, or
   108	vertices, which is what lets vouch, pile, and comms share it. Depends on
   109	`cryptography`, `pyjwt`, `python-ulid`; no internal loops deps.
   110	
   111	**custody** — Signing composition as code. One module, and the place where sign
   112	meets engine's injection points. Owns the domain-separation constants
   113	`loops-tick-v1`/`loops-fact-v1` (string-pinned here by Rule 8 — import them,
   114	never re-hardcode); the `keys/` custody layout co-located with the store it
   115	signs; `ensure_signing_key` as the single minting entry point; and the
   116	signer/verifier builders engine's callables expect. Engine never imports custody
   117	— injection, not import. Apps compose the two.
   118	
   119	**sdk** — Apex headless composition as code. The single composition point that
   120	unites atoms, store, lang, sign, custody, and engine into typed, headless
   121	operations (`declare`, `target`, `read`, `emit`, `kind`). Presentation surfaces
   122	(CLI commands, TUI shells, automation agents) consume `sdk` exclusively and contain
   123	zero substrate orchestration logic.
   124	
   125	**painted** — Lenses as code, and no longer in this repo. Terminal rendering
   126	primitives (Cell, Span, Line, Block, Style), composition (join, pad, border,
   127	truncate), and the `run_cli` harness that wires zoom levels and output modes into
   128	a standard CLI pattern. Lenses are functions: `(data, zoom, width) → Block`. It
   129	lives at [painted](https://github.com/kgruel/painted) and is consumed as a
   130	versioned PyPI dependency, pinned in the root `pyproject.toml`. Rendering changes
   131	happen upstream, not here.
   132	
   133	## Layers — the instrument families
   134	
   135	The libraries group into layers by **role** — which part of the communication
   136	problem each serves. The mapping follows Weaver's three levels of the
   137	communication problem (instrument-family survey, 2026-07-26; see
   138	`design:architecture/surfacing-layer-charter` in the project store):
   139	
   140	| Layer | Weaver level | Concern | Lib members |
   141	|-------|--------------|---------|-------------|
   142	| **record** | A — technical | What happened: accuracy of capture, storage, replay | atoms, lang, engine, store, migrate |
   143	| **view** | A — technical | Faithful presentation of what happened | painted (external repo) |
   144	| **surfacing** | C — effectiveness | Conduct and authority: host orchestration, coordination, attestation | sign, custody, sdk |
   145	| **relevance** | B — semantic | What matters: judgment over the record | *deliberately none — see below* |
   146	
   147	A layer is a **designation, not a package** — the record layer is four
   148	libraries, not one. Membership lives in this table and in the import DAG
   149	(`tests/test_architecture.py`, Rule 11); a new library must declare its
   150	layer or the suite fails. Apps take no layer assignment — they are
   151	compositions by construction (the loops CLI hosts record reads through view
   152	lenses under surfacing conduct). App-side surfacing occupants (the TUI
   153	shell, cross-implementation protocol artifacts) belong to the layer by role
   154	without being libs.
   155	
   156	**Why the record layer can make correctness claims:** it keeps Shannon's
   157	discipline of excluding semantics. FTS matches, it does not rank. Folds
   158	project, they do not judge. Accuracy is provable in the record layer
   159	precisely because meaning is out of scope there.
   160	
   161	**Surfacing is relational, not correct.** Host shells, protocol
   162	coordination, signing — these are reliable-or-not, conducted-well-or-not;
   163	they make claims about conduct and authority, not about truth. The layer
   164	exists as a *name* because its absence caused misfilings: with only record
   165	and view built, relation-shaped code got filed into the nearest substance —
   166	`run_cli`/`add_cli_args` into painted (a view home), signing composition
   167	into the CLI app before `custody` was carved out. The filing function
   168	failed for lack of a name; the name is the fix.
   169	
   170	**Relevance is deliberately homeless.** Judgments about what matters
   171	(ranking, staleness, salience) have so far dissolved into the substrate as
   172	*declarations* the record layer executes — lifecycle declarations were the
   173	first. Whether relevance becomes a package or stays a declaration
   174	vocabulary is decided on evidence, after the third declaration-executed
   175	judgment lands, not before. Until then nothing claims the relevance name.
   176	
   177	Two rules of motion:
   178	
   179	- **Record never imports surfacing.** Level A stays pure of Level C,
   180	  enforced structurally (Rule 11). Surfacing imports record freely — that
   181	  is the direction conduct faces the record.
   182	- **Relocation-on-pull.** Code moves into a layer's package when a consumer
   183	  pulls on the seam, never as a guilty refactor. The CLI-host extraction
   184	  from painted happens when the TUI shell pulls on it, gated as its own
   185	  painted-major coordination.
   186	
   187	## The Concrete Data Flow
   188	
   189	The paradigm says: observation → vertex → accumulate → boundary → tick. Here's
   190	what that looks like in this implementation:
   191	
   192	```
   193	Shell command (df -h, curl, docker ps, ...)
   194	      │
   195	      ▼
   196	   Source (atoms)
   197	      command → stdout → format (lines/json/ndjson/blob)
   198	      → parse pipeline [Split, Pick, Rename, Coerce]
   199	      → Fact(kind="disk", ts=now, payload={fs: "/dev/sda1", use: 50}, observer="monitor")
   200	      │
   201	      ▼
   202	   Vertex (engine)
   203	      │
   204	      ├── SqliteStore.append(fact)        # durable if configured
   205	      │
   206	      ├── route by fact.kind
   207	      │     ├── "disk"   → disk Loop     (Spec.apply, fold state)
   208	      │     ├── "health" → health Loop   (Spec.apply, fold state)
   209	      │     └── "deploy" → deploy Loop   (Spec.apply, fold state)
   210	      │
   211	      └── boundary check
   212	            │
   213	            ├── data-driven: fact.kind == boundary.kind → fire
   214	            ├── count-driven: N facts accumulated → fire
   215	            └── manual: vertex.tick("name", now) → fire all
   216	                  │
   217	                  ▼
   218	               Tick(name="disk", ts=now, payload={...}, origin="status")
   219	                  │
