    69	def test_record_layer_does_not_import_surfacing():
    70	    """Record-layer libs must not import surfacing-layer libs at runtime.
    71	
    72	    The record layer answers Weaver's Level A — what happened, with accuracy
    73	    claims that hold because meaning is out of scope there. The surfacing layer
    74	    is Level C: conduct and authority — host orchestration, coordination,
    75	    attestation. Those claims are relational (conducted-well-or-not), not
    76	    correctness claims. Level A stays semantically and structurally pure of
    77	    Level C, so a record-layer accuracy claim can never depend on an authority
    78	    judgment. The reverse direction is fine and expected: surfacing composes
    79	    over the record (custody -> engine), governed per-lib by Rule 4.
    80	
    81	    Rule 4 already forbids each specific edge this rule would catch today.
    82	    Rule 11 is the coarser overlay stated at the layer, so it survives future
    83	    edits to Rule 4's per-lib allowlist: widening ``_LIB_ALLOWED_RUNTIME`` for
    84	    a record lib is a one-line change that reads locally reasonable, and this
    85	    rule is what makes the layer inversion in it fail out loud.
    86	
    87	    TYPE_CHECKING-only imports are exempt, matching Rule 4 exactly — the
    88	    collector records runtime imports only, and an annotation-only reference
    89	    creates no runtime dependency to invert.
