    50	
    51	---
    52	
    53	## Level 1 — The 9 Pipeline Stages (§A–§I)
    54	
    55	1. **Stage 1 (§A) Legacy Source & Inventory**: Open legacy JSONL or SQLite source via `LegacySource`, scan and compute `SourceInventory` (fact counts, tick counts, observer census, first/last timestamps). Empty sources are refused.
    56	2. **Stage 2 (§B) Descriptor Resolution**: Derive the deterministic lineage identifier and target path (`<store_dir>/<lineage>.arrival`).
    57	3. **Stage 3 (§C) Transform Pipeline**: Apply deterministic transforms (e.g. `ulid_migration()`). Whole-unit drops are recorded in `TransformExceptions`; partial-batch drops are refused upstream.
    58	4. **Stage 4 (§D) Target Initialization & Preflight**: Pre-flight head journal permissions (`preflight_journal()`). Check for existing valid targets or resume staging logs safely (divergent logs are refused with no in-place repair).
    59	5. **Stage 5 (§E) Genesis & Key Introductions**: Mint ordinal 0 genesis introducing founding public key under arrival domain (`loops-arrival-v1`), followed by key introduction records for all declared observers.
    60	6. **Stage 6 (§F) Sequential Append**: Build outer-unsigned record drafts (`batch`, `fact`, `tick`) with deterministic SHA-256 hash chaining and append them sequentially.
    61	7. **Stage 7 (§G) Signed Migration Report**: Compile the comprehensive migration report (`<store_dir>/<lineage>.migration-report.json` beside target) containing source fingerprints, target head, and exceptions; verify that the injected signer returned a valid signature. (`verify_migration_report` exists separately for external auditor verification).
    62	8. **Stage 8 (§H) Inventory Equality**: Assert exact inventory equality between source inventory and target projection across all kinds and observers (accounting for whole-unit drops; record count comparison is deliberately excluded).
    63	9. **Stage 9 (§I) Atomic Descriptor Publish**: Verify the five code-level publish preconditions:
    64	   - `target_verify_full`: Target log verifies `Full(through=head)`
    65	   - `source_unchanged` (`SourceChangedRefused`): Legacy source content hash unchanged since snapshot
    66	   - `equivalence_rerun`: Re-derived expected rows match target records logical row-by-row
    67	   - `inventory_equality`: Target projection matches source inventory
    68	   - `journal_first_entry_mint`: First journal entry for lineage is `bootstrap`/`MINT`
    69	   (Note: The already-migrated guard is an apps-layer pre-flight in `loops/commands/store.py` (`_run_migrate`'s already-on-arrival pre-flight), not a `libs/migrate` precondition).
    70	   Then surgically update `.vertex` store clause using `lang.effective_store_clause` with verification-by-re-parse.
    71	
    72	---
    73	
    74	## Level 2 — Design Facts (F1–F7)
    75	
    76	For full architectural rationale and normative rulings, see `docs/architecture/arrival/protocol.html` §09, `backend-contract.html` §11, and `docs/scratch/arrival-break/slice4-design-proposal.md`:
    77	- **F1**: Lineage-named staging (`<store_dir>/<lineage>.arrival`) and resume idempotence
    78	- **F2**: Verification-by-re-parse for surgical `.vertex` descriptor edits
    79	- **F3**: Arrival custody domain separation (`loops-arrival-v1`)
    80	- **F4**: Outer-unsigned migrated records (content preserved verbatim, sealed by hash chain)
    81	- **F5**: Signed migration report stored beside target (`<store_dir>/<lineage>.migration-report.json`)
    82	- **F6**: 5 code-level publish preconditions (`target_verify_full`, `source_unchanged`, `equivalence_rerun`, `inventory_equality`, `journal_first_entry_mint`) and apps-layer already-migrated guard
    48	   - Source content hash unchanged since snapshot (two histories, not a migration).
    49	
    50	8. The migration report (§I.1, ratified M-3): `<lineage>.migration-report.json` beside
    51	   the target, signed by the custodian, referenced by nothing in the ledger.
    52	   Signing covers the canonical JSON bytes (RFC 8785 JCS) of the report body dictionary:
    53	   `canonical_bytes = _canonical_bytes(report_body)`
    54	   `digest = sha256(canonical_bytes).hexdigest()`
    55	   `sig = signer(custodian, digest)`
    56	   Report file format:
    57	   `{"body": report_body, "signature": sig, "signer": custodian}`
    58	
    59	9. Legacy store is never mutated and never deleted: Remains read-only archival evidence.
    60	"""
   962	            "target_rows": equiv_res.target_rows,
   963	            "mismatches": list(equiv_res.mismatches),
   964	        },
   965	        "custodian": genesis_req.custodian,
   966	    }
   967	
   968	    report_doc = {
   969	        "body": report_body,
   970	        "signer": genesis_req.custodian,
   971	    }
   972	    canonical_bytes = _canonical_bytes(report_doc)
   973	    digest = hashlib.sha256(canonical_bytes).hexdigest()
   974	    report_signature = signer(genesis_req.custodian, digest)
   975	    if report_signature is None:
   976	        raise PublishPreconditionRefused(
   977	            f"Custodian {genesis_req.custodian!r} produced no signature for migration report. "
   978	            "Advisory: check signer configuration.",
   979	            condition="report_signature",
   980	        )
   981	
   982	    report_document = {
    55	## Libraries
    56	
    57	Seven libraries in `libs/`. Each owns one concern. The runtime dependency graph is
    58	`_LIB_ALLOWED_RUNTIME` in `tests/test_architecture.py` — everything not listed
    59	there is forbidden, and the test is the authority:
    60	
    61	```
    62	atoms    ──→ ()
    63	lang     ──→ ()
    64	sign     ──→ ()
    65	engine   ──→ lang, atoms
    66	store    ──→ engine
    67	custody  ──→ sign, engine
    68	sdk      ──→ atoms, lang, sign, engine, store, custody
    69	```
    70	
    71	`X ──→ Y` reads *X imports Y at runtime*. painted is not in this graph — it
    72	lives in its own repo and arrives as a versioned PyPI dependency (see below).
   133	## Layers — the instrument families
   134	
   135	The libraries group into layers by **role** — which part of the communication
   136	problem each serves. The mapping follows Weaver's three levels of the
   137	communication problem (instrument-family survey, 2026-07-26; see
   138	`design:architecture/surfacing-layer-charter` in the project store):
   139	
   140	| Layer | Weaver level | Concern | Lib members |
   141	|-------|--------------|---------|-------------|
   142	| **record** | A — technical | What happened: accuracy of capture, storage, replay | atoms, lang, engine, store, migrate |
   143	| **view** | A — technical | Faithful presentation of what happened | painted (external repo) |
   144	| **surfacing** | C — effectiveness | Conduct and authority: host orchestration, coordination, attestation | sign, custody, sdk |
   145	| **relevance** | B — semantic | What matters: judgment over the record | *deliberately none — see below* |
   146	
   147	A layer is a **designation, not a package** — the record layer is four
   148	libraries, not one. Membership lives in this table and in the import DAG
   149	(`tests/test_architecture.py`, Rule 11); a new library must declare its
   150	layer or the suite fails. Apps take no layer assignment — they are
   151	compositions by construction (the loops CLI hosts record reads through view
   152	lenses under surfacing conduct). App-side surfacing occupants (the TUI
   153	shell, cross-implementation protocol artifacts) belong to the layer by role

diff --git a//tmp/sol-s4-r1/probe_cli_self_observer.py b//tmp/sol-s4-r1/probe_cli_self_observer.py
